l Round, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "40507"} "1904"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/21"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Double York Round, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "17 – 18 July 1908"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/216"} "White City Stadium, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/40523"} "27 from 3 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "72 arrows at 100 yards (91.4 metres), 48 arrows at 80 yards (73.2 metres), and 24 arrows at 60 yards (54.9 metres) on each day (288 arrows in all)."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "In this event, " [:a {:href "/athletes/575"} "William Dod"] " led after the first day, scoring 403 with 91 hits, to lead " [:a {:href "/athletes/572"} "Reginald Brooks-King"] ", who posted marks of 393 and 93.  " [:a {:href "/athletes/585"} "John Penrose"] " was 3rd after day one, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/2244"} "Henry Richardson"] " only in 5th place.  On Saturday, Richardson had the highest score, with 417 points and 93 hits, followed by Dod, who scored 412 with 94 hits.  However, Dod’s marks sufficed to keep him in the lead and earn him the gold medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "William Dod was certainly not favored.  Since 1900, the British National Championship had been divided among Brooks-King (5 titles - 1900, 1902-03, 1906, 1908), with John Penrose, " [:a {:href "/athletes/571"} "John Bridges"] ", Eyre Hussey, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/584"} "Hugh Nesham"] " winning a single championship."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Dod would win his only British title in 1909.  He was the brother of " [:a {:href "/athletes/536"} "Lottie Dod"] ", the runner-up in the " [:a {:href "/results/40528"} "women’s archery"] ", and likely the greatest female all-arounder in British sporting history.  They were appropriately descended from Sir Antony Dod, of Edge, who commanded the English archers at Agincourt.  From a wealthy family, William Dod never had to worry about school or work, and spent his time in the pursuit of his sporting interests.  He was also quite accomplished in golf and big game hunting."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Targets Hit"] [:th {} "Golds"] [:th {} "100 y"] [:th {} "80 y"] [:th {} "60 y"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/575"} "William Dod"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "815"] [:td {} "185"] [:td {} "13"] [:td {} "292 (1)"] [:td {} "299 (2)"] [:td {} "224 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/572"} "Reginald Brooks-King"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "768"] [:td {} "184"] [:td {} "7"] [:td {} "250 (4)"] [:td {} "300 (1)"] [:td {} "218 (4)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2244"} "Henry Richardson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-fla